# Inside a hole the LEXER reads `\"` as a string escape and copies both bytes
# through, while the hole splitter reads the copied `"` as a nested-string
# opener that never closes -- two readings of the same bytes, filed as R21d-4.
# Both compilers refuse it with this sentence. The supported spelling needs no
# escape at all: `f"a{"b"}"`, which tests/fstring_braces.ty exercises.
# Since 2026-09-25 the `\"` itself is refused by name, before either reading runs.
# expect: a `\"` inside an f-string hole
fn main():
    print(f"a{\"b}\n")
